---
description: Services and models must be passed explicitly to route factories, not looked up via app.locals
alwaysApply: true
---

# Service Injection Convention

All services and models that a route needs are passed in as **arguments to the route factory** in `server.js → setupRoutes()` and then closed over by the handlers. Route handlers MUST NOT reach into `req.app.locals.<thing>` to fetch a dependency at request time — that's a service-locator anti-pattern that hides the dependency graph.

This applies to `authService`, `userModel`, `pairingModel`, prompt services, `pushNotificationService`, and any future service.

## Pattern

```javascript
// server.js
app.use('/api/pairing', createPairingRoutes(pairingService, authService, pushNotificationService || null, userModel || null, pairingModel || null));

// routes/pairing.js
function createPairingRoutes(pairingService, authService, pushNotificationService = null, userModel = null, pairingModel = null) {
  router.post('/accept', authenticateToken, async (req, res) => {
    // ...
    if (pushNotificationService) {
      await pushNotificationService.sendToUser(userId, payload);
    }
  });
}
```

## Bad

```javascript
// ❌ Service-locator lookup at request time — hides the dependency,
//    breaks if server.js forgets to wire it, untestable in isolation.
router.post('/accept', authenticateToken, async (req, res) => {
  const push = req.app.locals.pushNotificationService;
  if (push) await push.sendToUser(userId, payload);
});
```

## Rules

- Optional dependencies default to `null` in the factory signature (e.g. `pushNotificationService = null`). Handlers null-check before calling.
- Do NOT add new `app.locals.X = X` assignments in `setupRoutes()` for dependencies. `app.locals` is for per-request middleware state, not the dependency graph.
- When extending an existing factory, append the new param to the end of the existing argument list to keep call sites stable.

## Known follow-up

`routes/users.js` still reads `req.app.locals.pairingModel` at line ~161. This is inherited from before this convention existed. Any PR that touches `routes/users.js` should opportunistically convert it: thread `pairingModel` through `createUserRoutes(...)` in `server.js` and drop the `app.locals.pairingModel = pairingModel;` assignment.
